.TH ACCORDIAN 6 "16 November 1992".SH NAMEaccordian\- the ``Accordian'' solitaire card game.SH SYNOPSIS.B accordian.SH DESCRIPTIONThe deck of cards is dealt either a card at a time, or theentire deck at once, depending on your playing style.  Aftercards are dealt, each card, and the stack underneath it, canbe moved either 1 or 3 spaces, onto another stack where thathas the same rank or suit for the topmost card in the stack..PPThe goal of the game is to move all the cards into a singlestack, containing all the cards..SH COMMANDS.RSSelect cards by typing their face value and suit.  Thecards are 2 to 9, J, Q, K, and A.  The suits are H, S, D, and C.If the card selected has only one choice for its move, it movesautomatically.  You will be prompted for your move, if you havea choice between moving the card 1 or 3 spaces..IP `d'Deal a single card from the deck..IP `D'Deal the remainder of the deck..IP `f'Find the first card that is still able to move..IP `s'Save the current deck into memory, so it can be restored later.This is the only way to `redo' moves you have undone..IP `r'Restore the most recently saved copy of the deck..IP `u'Undo the last single move.  `Undo' will save all the previous movesthat you have played, except for saving and restoring the deck..IP `x'Exit from Accordian..IP `^R'Redraw the screen.IP `?'Print a screen of instructions and help..RE.SH OPTIONS.RSIf any command line options are specified,.B accordianwill print the same help as found by typing `?' while playing the game..RE.SH HISTORYAccordian was originally written during Summer 1990.  Minor enhancementswere made in March 1991, and November 1992..SH BUGSThe game is almost impossible to win.  This is a bug in thegame, not the implementation.  :-).SH AUTHOREric Lechner, ericl@xilinx.com